On December 12, the eighth GAIR Global Artificial Intelligence and Robotics Summit officially commenced in Shenzhen. The two-day event was jointly organized by the GAIR Research Institute and Leiphone, with Academician Gao Wen serving as the chair of the steering committee, and Academician Yang Qiang and Professor Zhu Xiaorui as the conference chairs. As a benchmark gathering for the AI industry, academia, research, and investment, GAIR has consistently upheld the core principle of "heritage + innovation" since its inception in 2016. It serves as a platform for intellectual exchange in the AI academic community, a hub for technical discussions, and a spiritual home for China's four-decade journey in AI development. Over the past four years, large models have accelerated the transformation of the AI industry. As the year draws to a close, GAIR returns as scheduled, fostering high-quality idea exchanges and presenting cutting-edge insights into the AI era for the industry and the public. At this summit, Professor Jia Jiaya, Chair Professor at the Hong Kong University of Science and Technology, Director of the Von Neumann Institute, and IEEE Fellow, delivered an engaging opening keynote. Professor Jia first reviewed the AI research and achievements led by his team before and after the establishment of the Von Neumann Institute. Some of the latest technological breakthroughs from this year, previously undisclosed in public, include:  
1. In 2023, the release of LongLoRA technology, making it the world's first large model technology for 32K long-text context understanding;  
2. In 2024, the launch of Mini-Gemini—a platform that has garnered over 3,000 stars on GitHub.
